  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes the cellular organization of inflamed synovium and highlights why synovial cell populations can be heterogeneous. The tissue is divided into a surface lining layer and a deeper sub-lining layer. Different synoviocyte subsets are shown by color and shape: a non-pathogenic type, a joint-destructive type, and an immune-effector type. In the lining layer, cells appear densely packed, with destructive synoviocytes prominent among the surface cells. In the sub-lining layer, immune-effector and non-pathogenic synoviocytes are intermingled with many infiltrating leukocytes and small blood vessels, suggesting active immune cell recruitment and local inflammation.\n\nThe overall message is that synovial tissue contains multiple fibroblast-like cell states with distinct roles, distributed differently across tissue layers, and interacting with immune cells and vasculature. A callout on the right indicates that observed cell heterogeneity is shaped by several non-cell-intrinsic factors, including treatment, disease stage, sample type, disease duration, and autoantibody presence. Together, the figure conveys that synovial pathology is not driven by a single uniform cell population, but by a layered and variable microenvironment in which destructive and immune-associated stromal cells coexist with infiltrating leukocytes.",
